o.mod:14`, `gcp/observability/go.mod:34`, `security/advancedtls/go.mod:14`, `security/advancedtls/examples/go.mod:14`, `interop/observability/go.mod:34` | `golang.org/x/net v0.12.0 // indirect` | Indirect |\n| grpc-go | `/workspace/grpc-go/examples/features/observability/go.mod:31` | `golang.org/x/net v0.8.0 // indirect` | Indirect (stale sub-module, superseded by root's 0.12.0 in a joint build) |\n\nNo `replace` directives override `golang.org/x/net` in any repo/sub-module — the `go.mod` requirements above are authoritative (confirmed by search: `replace` blocks present in these repos only redirect internal multi-module paths, e.g. `k8s.io/api => ../api`, `go.etcd.io/etcd => ./FORBIDDEN_DEPENDENCY`, `google.golang.org/grpc => ../..`).\n\n## 3. Version Analysis — Resolved (go.sum) Versions vs. Fix Threshold (0.17.0)\n\n| Repo (module) | Resolved `golang.org/x/net` (go.sum `h1:`) | Vulnerable? |\n|---|---|---|\n| kubernetes (root, `/workspace/kubernetes/go.sum:1012-1013`) | **v0.13.0** | **Yes** (< 0.17.0) |\n| etcd (root, `/workspace/etcd/go.sum:387-388`) | **v0.7.0** | **Yes** |\n| etcd (`server`, `/workspace/etcd/server/go.sum:377-378`) | **v0.7.0** | **Yes** |\n| grpc-go (root, `/workspace/grpc-go/go.sum:54-55`) | **v0.12.0** | **Yes** |\n\n**All three repositories resolve to a `golang.org/x/net` version before the 0.17.0 fix and are therefore vulnerable to CVE-2023-39325 at the dependency-version level.** Because Go modules are resolved per top-level module (and `go mod vendor` flattens to one version per vendored tree), every sub-module/staging package inside a given repo that declares an *indirect* requirement on `golang.org/x/net` shares that repo's single resolved version — there is no version skew within a repo.\n\n## 4. Transitive Paths & Actual Exploitability (Dependency DAG)\n\nResolving to a vulnerable version is necessary but not sufficient — exploitability depends on whether the vulnerable code path (`http2.Server`/`http2.Transport`'s stream-reset accounting) is actually reachable. Blast radius differs sharply per repo:\n\n### 4.1 kubernetes — Directly Exploitable (kube-apiserver)\n\n- `/workspace/kubernetes/staging/src/k8s.io/apiserver/pkg/server/secure_serving.go:31` imports `golang.org/x/net/http2` directly and, at `secure_serving.go:200`, calls **`http2.ConfigureServer(secureServer, http2Options)`** to enable HTTP/2 on the kube-apiserver's secure serving `net/http.Server`. TLS config at `secure_serving.go:52` sets `NextProtos: []string{\"h2\", \"http/1.1\"}`.\n  - This is the **primary direct attack surface**: any HTTP/2 client (authenticated or not, depending on network exposure) can open a TLS connection to the API server and mount a Rapid Reset flood against `x/net/http2.Server`.\n- `/workspace/kubernetes/staging/src/k8s.io/client-go/rest/request.go:37` imports `golang.org/x/net/http2` and handles `http2.StreamError` (`request.go:1102`) — client-side HTTP/2 usage for streaming subresources (`exec`/`attach`/`port-forward`).\n- `/workspace/kubernetes/staging/src/k8s.io/apimachinery/pkg/util/net/http.go:39` imports `golang.org/x/net/http2` and, at `http.go:175`, calls **`http2.ConfigureTransports(t)`** — this configures the HTTP/2 `Transport` used by all client-go-derived clients (kubectl, controllers, etc.), a secondary/lower-severity path (malicious API server could target a client `Transport`).\n- `/workspace/kubernetes/vendor/golang.org/x/net/http2/*.go` — the vendored copy confirms the flattened build-wide version is **v0.13.0** (matches go.sum).\n- **Notable transitive detail:** Kubernetes vendors etcd's server code directly at `/workspace/kubernetes/vendor/go.etcd.io/etcd/server/v3/embed/serve.go:262`, which itself calls `http2.ConfigureServer(srv, &http2.Server{MaxConcurrentStreams: cfg.MaxConcurrentStreams})`. Because Go's module vendoring flattens all deps to one version per build, this vendored etcd code runs against **kubernetes's own resolved x/net v0.13.0**, not etcd upstream's v0.7.0 — it is a second, independent invocation of the vulnerable API inside the same binary graph, reachable only if that vendored package is actually linked into a k8s binary.\n\n### 4.2 etcd — Directly Exploitable (client/peer HTTP endpoints and gRPC-proxy)\n\n- `/workspace/etcd/server/embed/serve.go:45` imports `golang.org/x/net/http2`; at `serve.go:262` calls **`http2.ConfigureServer(srv, &http2.Server{MaxConcurrentStreams: cfg.MaxConcurrentStreams})`** to enable HTTP/2 on etcd's client-facing HTTP listener (serves the gRPC-gateway/JSON API and metrics alongside gRPC). This is a **direct attack surface** on any etcd member exposing its client URL.\n- `/workspace/etcd/server/etcdmain/grpc_proxy.go:51` imports `golang.org/x/net/http2`; at `grpc_proxy.go:233` calls **`http2.ConfigureServer(srvhttp, &http2.Server{MaxConcurrentStreams: maxConcurrentStreams})`** — the standalone `etcd grpc-proxy` binary's HTTP/2 front end is likewise directly exploitable.\n- `/workspace/etcd/server/proxy/httpproxy/proxy.go:24` imports `golang.org/x/net/http2` (legacy v2 HTTP proxy path).\n- `/workspace/etcd/client/pkg/transport/listener.go:588-590` references `golang.org/x/net/http2/http2.go`'s `IsClosedConnError` logic (copied, not imported) — informational only, not a live dependency edge.\n- **gRPC transitive path:** etcd's client and server RPC communication is layered on `google.golang.org/grpc` (see etcd's `go.mod`), which itself depends on `golang.org/x/net` (see §4.3) for framing/HPACK primitives only — etcd does not gain *additional* x/net-Server exposure through grpc-go beyond what grpc-go itself has, because grpc-go's stream handling bypasses `x/net/http2.Server`.\n\n### 4.3 grpc-go — x/net Dependency Present but NOT the Vulnerable Code Path; Separate grpc-go-Native Rapid Reset Issue Applies\n\n- grpc-go imports `golang.org/x/net/http2` and `golang.org/x/net/http2/hpack` in its production RPC transport: `/workspace/grpc-go/internal/transport/http2_client.go:35-36`, `/workspace/grpc-go/internal/transport/http2_server.go:36-37`, `/workspace/grpc-go/internal/transport/http_util.go:38-39`, `/workspace/grpc-go/internal/transport/controlbuf.go:31-32`, `/workspace/grpc-go/internal/transport/handler_server.go:39`.\n- **Critically, grpc-go does not construct or run an `x/net/http2.Server` or `http2.Transport` for actual RPC serving.** It uses `golang.org/x/net/http2` only for shared types/constants (`http2.Setting`, `http2.SettingMaxConcurrentStreams` at `http2_server.go:181` and `http2_client.go:1204`, `http2.ErrCode`, frame constants) and `golang.org/x/net/http2/hpack` for header (de)compression. The actual HTTP/2 frame loop, stream table, and `RST_STREAM` accounting are **grpc-go's own hand-written implementation** in `internal/transport/http2_server.go` / `http2_client.go` — entirely separate code from the vulnerable logic patched in x/net 0.17.0.\n  - Consequence: **bumping `golang.org/x/net` to ≥0.17.0 alone does not remediate Rapid Reset risk for grpc-go's RPC path**, because that path was never running the patched code.\n  - The only place grpc-go actually instantiates a real `http2.Server{...}` + `http2.ConfigureServer(...)` is in **test infrastructure** (`/workspace/grpc-go/test/end2end_test.go:674`, `test/servertester.go`, `test/rawConnWrapper.go`) and `handler_server.go` relies on the *standard library's* HTTP/2 support (via `net/http`), not directly on the `x/net` module build.\n- **This repo's actual Rapid Reset exposure is tracked by grpc-go's own advisory (same CVE-2023-44487 attack class, distinct code fix), remediated in grpc-go releases v1.56.3 / v1.57.1 / v1.58.3** (added explicit enforcement against excessive stream resets in `internal/transport/http2_server.go`). This workspace pins **v1.58.0**, which predates v1.58.3 — so grpc-go itself, independent of the x/net version question, needs an upgrade to a patched grpc-go release.\n- Both kubernetes and etcd depend on `google.golang.org/grpc` for their internal gRPC traffic (kube-apiserver, etcd client/server, etcd grpc-proxy) — so this grpc-go-native issue is a second, parallel remediation item riding on the same attack technique, layered underneath the x/net-specific fix.\n\n## 5. Summary — Full Dependency DAG\n\n```\n                     CVE-2023-39325 (golang.org/x/net/http2 < 0.17.0)\n                                      │\n      ┌───────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────┐\n      │                               │                               │\n  kubernetes                        etcd                          grpc-go\n  x/net v0.13.0 (direct,          x/net v0.7.0 (direct in         x/net v0.12.0 (direct)\n  go.mod:82; go.sum:1012)         server/go.mod:39;               go.mod:14; go.sum:54\n      │                            go.sum:387)                        │\n      ├─ apiserver/secure_serving.go   │                               ├─ internal/transport/http2_{client,server}.go\n      │  http2.ConfigureServer()       ├─ server/embed/serve.go        │  → uses x/net/http2 for Setting/ErrCode\n      │  → DIRECT server exposure      │  http2.ConfigureServer()      │    constants + hpack only; own frame loop\n      │                                │  → DIRECT client-URL exposure │  → x/net 0.17.0 bump does NOT fix this path\n      ├─ client-go/rest + apimachinery │                               │\n      │  util/net (ConfigureTransports)├─ server/etcdmain/grpc_proxy.go│  → SEPARATE grpc-go-native Rapid Reset\n      │  → client-side Transport       │  http2.ConfigureServer()      │    fix needed: upgrade to grpc-go\n      │                                │  → DIRECT grpc-proxy exposure │    ≥ v1.56.3 / v1.57.1 / v1.58.3\n      └─ vendors etcd/server/v3/embed  │                               │\n         (vendor/go.etcd.io/etcd/...)  └─ uses google.golang.org/grpc  └─ depended on by both kubernetes and\n         → runs 2nd http2.ConfigureServer  for client/server RPC          etcd for internal RPC transport\n           call under k8s's OWN            (inherits grpc-go's native\n           x/net v0.13.0 build               issue, not an x/net one)\n```\n\n## 6. Remediation Checklist\n\n1. **kubernetes**: bump `golang.org/x/net` to ≥ v0.17.0 in root `go.mod` and re-run `go mod vendor` (propagates to all 19 staging modules and the vendor tree) — closes the direct kube-apiserver `http2.ConfigureServer` exposure and the client-go `Transport` exposure.\n2. **etcd**: bump `golang.org/x/net` to ≥ v0.17.0 in root `go.mod` and `server/go.mod` (and transitively `api`, `pkg`, `client/v3`, `etcdctl`, `etcdutl`, `tests`) — closes the `embed/serve.go` and `etcdmain/grpc_proxy.go` direct exposures.\n3. **grpc-go**: bump `golang.org/x/net` to ≥ v0.17.0 **and, separately and more importantly, upgrade grpc-go itself to ≥ v1.58.3** (or backport its Rapid Reset stream-reset guard) — the x/net bump alone is insufficient for this repo's actual RPC serving path.\n4. Because kubernetes and etcd both consume `google.golang.org/grpc`, step 3's grpc-go upgrade must also be picked up by kubernetes' and etcd's own `go.mod` requirements on `google.golang.org/grpc` to fully close the grpc-go-native issue in those two repos' RPC layers.\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false}}
